#!/bin/sh

[ "${ACTION}" = "add" ] || return 0

# Nothing to generate on a box that is not part of a mesh.
#
# This handler fires on every radio that appears, at every boot, whether or not
# map-agent is enabled and whether or not it is even running - which is what
# makes it easy to miss. Measured 2026-08-17 on a factory-fresh production
# image: with mapagent and mapcontroller both disabled and every mesh worker
# switched off, the box still came up with the per-radio defaults rewritten
# into one MLD and a backhaul on the air. The OpenWrt-2g/5g/6g networks the
# board is meant to offer were gone, and a backhaul nobody had asked for was
# broadcasting - on a router whose owner may never want a mesh at all.
#
# It is also why those networks looked unpredictable: the rewrite lands in UCI
# after hostapd has already started, so config and air disagree until something
# reloads wifi, and then the backhaul appears out of nowhere.
#
# The test is what someone DECIDED, never what they named things. /etc/mapc/role
# is written by easymesh-role before it touches anything else, and every way
# into the mesh goes through it. A box configured before that marker existed
# carries dhcp.lan.ignore instead - '0' on a controller, '1' on an agent, unset
# from the factory - which easymesh-role is the only thing that writes.
#
# Measured on three boxes at once, and every other candidate failed: a fresh
# box already had /etc/mapc/adopt-state, already had mapc.db, and already had
# controller_select.local='0' because map-agent elects a controller by itself
# when it sees one. An earlier version of this file tested the fronthaul SSID
# against a list of factory names - which would have silenced the mesh of
# anyone who called their network "OpenWrt-home".

# A join in progress counts too, and this is the half the first version got
# wrong. easymesh-role writes /etc/mapc/role only in phase 2, but the join
# needs its config built before that: WPS delivers the backhaul key into
# mapagent's ap sections, and if those do not exist the key is written nowhere.
# Measured 2026-08-17: WPS-SUCCESS on the controller, and on the joining box no
# ap sections at all, no credentials, and bh-key-adopt waiting for a key that
# had been delivered an hour earlier. /etc/easymesh-wps-pending is written by
# phase 1 and removed when the join finishes or fails, which is exactly the
# window in which genconfig must be allowed to work.
#
# 2026-08-17, later the same day: dhcp.lan.ignore was removed from this test.
# It had been included as the mark carried by nodes configured before
# /etc/mapc/role existed. It cannot serve that purpose - easymesh-dhcp-safe
# writes dhcp.lan.ignore='1' on every box that is not a controller, factory ones
# included, so the mark appeared on untouched routers and reopened this gate one
# boot later. Three builds in a row came up with a backhaul on the air because of
# it. Older nodes now get the real marker from 88-easymesh-role-marker instead.
#
# A mark only means "someone decided" if nothing but a decision can produce it.
[ -f /etc/mapc/role ] ||
	[ -f /etc/easymesh-wps-pending ] || return 0

/lib/wifi/multiap conf
